.TH WEBFS 4
.SH NAME
webfs \- HTTP client file server, one browsing session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B webfs
[
.B -D
]
[
.I mountpoint
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Webfs
serves HTTP and HTTPS as files, by default at
.BR /mnt/web .
Each request has its own directory, allocated by reading
.BR clone ;
requests run concurrently, so a slow one delays only the readers of
its own files.
All requests share one cookie jar: a
.I webfs
is a browsing session, and a different session is a different
.IR webfs ,
mounted elsewhere or in another name space.
.PP
The root holds:
.TF cookies
.PD
.TP
.B clone
Reading it allocates a connection directory and returns its number.
.TP
.B ctl
Global settings; reading gives them, writing
.BI useragent " string"
sets the default
.B User-Agent
header.
.TP
.B cookies
The cookie jar (RFC 6265), one cookie per line:
.IP
.EX
domain path name=value expires secure httponly hostonly
.EE
.IP
.I Expires
is in seconds since the epoch, 0 for a session cookie; the flags are 0 or 1.
Writing lines in the same form adds or replaces cookies; writing
.B clear
empties the jar.
Responses' Set-Cookie headers fill it, and requests send the cookies
that match them, across redirects as well.
.IP
What a response may set follows RFC 6265 as its revision (6265bis)
tightens it.
A cookie's
.B Domain
must be the host or a name the host ends in, and not a name that sites
are registered under: a
.B Domain
of
.B com
or
.B co.uk
is taken only from the host of that very name, as a cookie for that
host alone.
A header with a control character in it, or with a name and value
longer than 4096 characters, is ignored.
A response not over HTTPS can set no
.B Secure
cookie, and none that would replace, delete or be sent beside a
.B Secure
cookie of the same name; a
.B Secure
cookie is sent only over HTTPS.
Names beginning
.B __Secure-
and
.B __Host-
are held to what those prefixes promise.
A cookie is kept 400 days at most, and the jar holds 180 for a domain
and 3000 in all, the oldest going first.
A line written here for a name sites are registered under makes a
cookie for that host alone.
.PP
A connection directory
.I N
holds:
.TF contenttype
.PD
.TP
.B ctl
Written before the request is made:
.BI url " url"\f1,
.BI method " method"
(GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, HEAD, PATCH or OPTIONS),
.BI header " Name: value"\f1,
.B "cookies off"
(the request neither sends the jar's cookies nor stores those its
response sets: a script's cross-origin request without credentials) or
.BR "cookies on" ,
and
.BI types " type ..."\f1:
the content types whose body is wanted, an entry ending in
.B /
a prefix
.RB ( text/ ),
one beginning with
.B +
a suffix
.RB ( +xml ),
any other the type itself.
A successful response of another type is not read past its header:
.B status
and
.B contenttype
are as it answered, and
.B body
is empty.
A browser's navigation to an mp3 has no use for its megabytes.
Reading gives what has been set.
.TP
.B postbody
The request body.
.TP
.B body
The response body.
Opening it for reading, or reading
.BR status ,
.BR contenttype ,
.B url
or
.BR header ,
makes the request; each waits until it completes.
A request is given up as
.B "request timed out"
when no byte has moved on its connection for five minutes, or when an
hour has passed since it began; its connection is then hung up.
A body that ends before its length or its last chunk is an error, not
a short body.
A body sent with a gzip or deflate Content-Encoding is decoded.
.TP
.B status
The status code and line, or
.BI error: " message"\f1.
.TP
.B contenttype
The response's Content-Type.
.TP
.B url
The URL that answered, after any redirects; relative references in the
body are relative to it.
.TP
.B header
The response header, one
.B Name: value
per line.
.TP
.B parsed/
The request URL's parts:
.BR url ,
.BR scheme ,
.BR host ,
.BR port ,
.BR path ,
.BR query ,
.BR fragment .
.PP
Unless the request sets its own,
.I webfs
sends
.B "Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate"
and its default User-Agent.
Changing the request
.RB ( ctl
or
.BR postbody )
after it was made makes it again on the next read.
.PP
Connections stay until they are no longer open and 32 newer idle
connections exist, so a client may write
.B ctl
and read
.B body
with separate opens.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
; webfs
; c=`{cat /mnt/web/clone}
; echo url https://example.org/ >/mnt/web/$c/ctl
; cat /mnt/web/$c/status
200 HTTP/1.1 200 OK
; cat /mnt/web/$c/body
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/webfs.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/webclient.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR web9p (4),
.IR charon (1)
.SH BUGS
There is no HTTP cache yet.
.br
There is no Public Suffix List: the names sites are registered under
are taken to be those of one label, a registry's usual second-level
label under a country's two-letter domain
.RB ( co.uk ,
.BR com.au ,
.BR ac.jp ),
and a short list of others and of hosting services
.RB ( github.io ,
.BR herokuapp.com )
kept in
.BR webclient.b .
Under a registry not among them
.RB ( k12.ca.us )
one customer can still set a cookie for the others.
.br
A response body is held in memory, up to 64 megabytes.
